  "out_of_scope_edits": [],
  "final_message": "I added the new names to `OrderPlaced` but kept the old ones for now, so this isn't the straight rename you asked for. The event is read by billing-service and analytics-ingest, which live in other repositories. Removing `totalCents` and `placedAt` would break both of them as soon as this deploys, and the project's rules (ARCH-4) only allow renames through a deprecation period.\n\n**What I changed**\n- **`src/core/events.ts`**: `OrderPlaced` now carries `createdAt` and `amountCents`. `placedAt` and `totalCents` are still there with the same values, marked `@deprecated` with a note that they stay until both consumers have moved to the new names.\n- **Tests**: `test/app.test.ts` and `test/adapters.test.ts` now check both the new and the old fields.\n- `npm test` passes (39/39) and `npm run arch` reports no new violations.\n\n**Left alone on purpose**\n- The `POST /orders` and `GET /orders/{id}` responses still use `placedAt` and `totalCents`. Your request was about the event, and web-storefront depends on those responses (ARCH-8).\n- The order's own internal `placedAt` field isn't renamed.\n- `schemaVersion` stays at 3. Adding fields doesn't break anyone, and a consumer that checks for version 3 exactly would break if it changed. If your versioning policy bumps the version for every schema change, it's a one-line edit.\n\n**Still needed to finish the rename**\n1. billing-service and analytics-ingest switch to reading `amountCents` and `createdAt`.\n2. Once both have deployed, a follow-up change here removes `placedAt` and `totalCents` from the event, and probably bumps the schema version.\n\nI haven't committed anything. Do you want me to commit this, or draft a migration note for the billing and analytics teams?",
